  "step_3": "The decision reason requires (1) computing an aspiration level as the mean of all outcomes (ignoring probabilities), and (2) preferring the lottery with the higher chance of exceeding this level. The individual does not mention any such aspiration level, nor do they refer to comparing outcomes to a reference point of 3000. Instead, they explicitly refer to expected value (which they compute correctly) and then justify their choice based on risk aversion and preference for safer, smaller gains. Their reasoning aligns more with a 'satisficing' or risk-minimization heuristic rather than the specific aspiration-level rule described. There is no evidence that they considered whether outcomes exceed 3000 or evaluated the probabilities of such exceedances. The information considered (expected value, risk level, safety) does not match the information required by the reason (aspiration level, positive deviations). The justification (risk conservatism) differs from the reason’s rule (maximize chance of positive deviation from aspiration).",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
